<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelo Telefone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Pelo Telefone&quot; (&quot;On the Telephone&quot;) is held, according to the records of the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, to be the first samba song recorded in Brazil, although earlier recordings exist, such as &quot;Samba – Em Casa da Bahiana&quot; (1913) and &quot;Urubu Malandro&quot; (1914).</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song was composed in 1916 in the backyard of Tia Ciata, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> house in Praça Onze, now Cidade Nova, was frequented by the popular musicians of the time. It was a collective creation, first titled Roceiro, made with João da Baiana, Pixinguinha, Caninha, Hilário Jovino Ferreira, Sinhô and others, and it grew out of improvisation in a samba circle.</p><p>On 27 November 1916 Ernesto dos Santos, known as Donga, registered it as his work alone; he later added the journalist Mauro de Almeida as a partner. Donga justified registering it first with a saying attributed to Sinhô: music is like a bird, it belongs to whoever catches it first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The original words were later changed into the version best known today. In a statement to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s Museum of Image and Sound, Donga said that the lines about the chief of police were a parody made by the journalists of the newspaper A Noite. In 1913 its reporters had placed a roulette wheel in Largo da Carioca to show how far the police tolerated gambling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song was a huge success, and because it was born of joint improvisation various musicians claimed its authorship. In the newspaper O Dia of 13 February 1972 the musician and broadcaster Henrique Foréis Domingues confirmed the origin of the parody and accused Donga of appropriating a collective work.</p><p>Donga maintained that the music was different, but conceded that the words were Mauro de Almeida's and blamed the label Casa Edison for leaving his partner's name off the recording. As early as 4 February 1917 the Jornal do Brasil printed a notice from Grêmio Fala Gente announcing the true tango Pelo Telephone, by the composers João da Mata, Germano, Tia Ciata and Hilário.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pelo Telefone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Pelo Telefone</b>&quot; (&quot;On the Telephone&quot;) is held, according to the records of the National Library of Brazil, to be the first samba song recorded in Brazil, although earlier recordings exist, such as &quot;Samba – Em Casa da Bahiana&quot; (1913) and &quot;Urubu Malandro&quot; (1914).</blockquote><a href="/songs/pelo-telefone">Pelo Telefone</a></li></ul></section>
